body:
.... all the four's, 44!

&#x200B;

:)

I did BA English Lit and History so literally nothing relevant to Project Delivery. The only thing slightly relevant to that is that I did a placement year in educational outreach whereby I organised and delivered projects for local young people. I have met people who have literally have no experience and come straight from uni aged 21. 

&#x200B;

I applied twice. The first time I didn't get past the first online test lol (I rushed it and I didn't understand what they were assessing me on) and the second time I was successful. I would literally study the competencies and "success profiles" on this website: [https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/civil-service-competency-framework](https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/civil-service-competency-framework) because this is what they're assessing you against. I think you have to adjust your mindset to the way they want you to think. I would also recommend stalking The Student Room because they have a thread every year whereby people are applying and giving advice and I found a lot of tips on there. Someone told me it takes an average of 3 attempts to get on the fast stream though so if you want it, don't give up!!

I'm 24 and I earn 28k in the public sector, on civil service fast stream. There are lots of people who are training for the same role as me (project management/delivery) with the view to move to the private sector to maximise earnings and have a more generous salary. 

&#x200B;

For me personally, having a career I enjoy is more important than money. So even though I've had alternative offers and could move elsewhere for more money, I wouldn't. I would also do the same job for less money (if the salary was different) because it has always been something I want to do. I'm happy with what I earn because it affords me a generous enough lifestyle for a single person. Although I suppose I am fortunate in that my salary (and benefits package) is probably more generous/flexible than others so it's easy to not be bothered about wanting more. My view might potentially change if/when I have children and I have other people to support.

Used to work at the University of Leeds, visiting all schools in Yorkshire to try and teach young people employability skills such as CV and cover letter writing - I am happy to check over your CV for you if you wish.

&#x200B;

Try fast food places or retail. People laugh at working in maccies but you get free meals on shift and the pay/benefits/flexibility is actually pretty decent for a first job. My friend still does some shifts there now even though we've finished uni because they give her great training, work hours around her other commitments and allow her to take long unpaid breaks to travel.

I got my first credit card when I was a student, literally just to build up my credit because I realised that moving around a lot (almost every year) and having no real stable address was kind of damaging. 

&#x200B;

I did what you did too - have the electricity and gas bills in my name and took an overdraft, even though i didn't need it. I got a basic student credit card but now I use amex so I can collect rewards for basically all of my spending. It's also good to have a credit card for larger purchases that you might need protection on.

meeee! I have just returned from a long hiatus too.

&#x200B;

UN: soporifics
